{# The landing page, at /v2/python/ and /v2/python/index.html. #}
{% extends "frame.html.jinja2" %}
{% block title %}pycarlo2 SDK reference{% endblock %}
{% block nav %}{% include "nav.html.jinja2" %}{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
    <main class="pdoc">
        <section class="module-info">
            <h1 class="modulename">pycarlo2 SDK reference</h1>
            <div class="docstring">
                <p>Version: {{ docs_version }}</p>
                <p><code>pycarlo2</code> lets you manage your Monte Carlo account from Python:
                    connections, warehouses, agents and the rest of the REST API. Install it, set up
                    credentials, then find any operation below. The
                    <a href="../index.html">REST API reference</a> describes each operation's
                    requests and responses.</p>

                <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
                <pre><code>pip install git+https://github.com/monte-carlo-data/mc-sdk-python.git</code></pre>
                <p>Requires Python 3.10 or later.</p>

                <h2 id="getting-started">Getting started</h2>
                <p>Set up credentials with one of the guides:</p>
                <ul>
                    {% for g in guides %}
                        <li><a href="{{ g.file }}">{{ g.title }}</a></li>
                    {% endfor %}
                </ul>
                <p>Then build a client and check who it acts as:</p>
                <pre><code class="language-python">import pycarlo2

client = pycarlo2.new_client()
print(pycarlo2.UsersApi(client).get_current_user())</code></pre>
                <p><a href="pycarlo2/auth.html#new_client"><code>new_client</code></a> takes its
                    credentials from <a href="pycarlo2/auth.html#Options"><code>Options</code></a>
                    first, then the <code>MCD_DEFAULT_*</code> environment variables, then the
                    profile the <code>montecarlo</code> CLI writes to
                    <code>~/.mcd/profiles.ini</code>. A misconfiguration raises
                    <code>pycarlo2.CredentialsError</code> there, and a failed call raises
                    <code>pycarlo2.ApiException</code>.</p>
                <p>Every operation below is a method on an <code>*Api</code> class built from the
                    client, and takes and returns the classes listed under Models. All of them are
                    importable from <code>pycarlo2</code>.</p>

                <h2 id="paginate">Walk a paginated list</h2>
                <p>A list returns one page per call. <a href="pycarlo2/paging.html#paginate"><code>paginate</code></a>
                    follows the cursor and yields every item:</p>
                <pre><code class="language-python">from pycarlo2.paging import paginate

for warehouse in paginate(pycarlo2.WarehousesApi(client).list_warehouses):
    print(warehouse.id, warehouse.name)</code></pre>

                <h2 id="operations">Operations</h2>
                {% for api in operations %}
                    <h3 id="{{ api.name }}"><a href="{{ api.page }}">{{ api.name }}</a></h3>
                    <table>
                        {% for op in api.operations %}
                            <tr>
                                <td><a href="{{ api.page }}#{{ api.name }}.{{ op.name }}"><code>{{ op.name }}</code></a></td>
                                <td>{{ op.summary }}</td>
                            </tr>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </table>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
{% endblock %}
